 "transcript": [
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "my washin mashine wont turn on at all no lights nothin"
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "From what you have described, the most likely cause is property socket / supply / switched fused spur. Next: Check the socket works with another appliance and that any separate switched/fused spur controlling it is on (especially after a recent move). Let me know what you find."
  },
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "the kettle works in the kitchen so the sockets are fine i think. whats a spur? i dont really know what that is"
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "A spur is a dedicated electrical circuit point, often used for high-power appliances like washing machines or cookers. It typically involves a fused connection unit with a 13-amp fuse to protect the wiring. Next: Check the plug is fully seated and the lead undamaged - vibration can work a plug loose over time. Let me know what you find."
  },
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "i cant get to the plug its behind the machine and its built in so i cant pull it out. is there anything else i can check?"
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "Since you have confirmed the socket works, the issue likely lies with the appliance’s internal power supply or a tripped safety device. You should check if your home’s fuse box has tripped for that circuit and ensure the washing machine's plug is firmly inserted behind it. Do other nearby sockets have power, and has the machine or your home recently been moved or had a power cut?"
  },
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "yeah the other sockets in the kitchen work, kettle and fridge are fine. all the switches in the fuse box are up. no power cuts that i know of, it worked 2 days ago. not sure about it being moved, its built in"
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "From what you have checked, this needs an appliance engineer to look at it: the next steps are beyond the checks that are safe to do yourself."
  }
 ],
